# The ORDERED-FIELD steps of hyperbolic geometry: the inequalities that turn the identities of
# space/hyperbolic/metric into the theorems they are about. metric proves, as polynomial identities, that the Gram
# determinant of three points is a square and that it equals (p^2 - 1)(q^2 - 1) - (r - p q)^2. What is left is
# ORDER: from a squared inequality to the inequality, from a product's sign to its factors' signs, from a
# non-negative discriminant to a meeting point. Each rule below is proved by the product prover (check/product.ts), a
# degree-two Positivstellensatz: the hypotheses and the negated goal are multiplied in pairs (and with squares and
# monomials), and an exact simplex finds non-negative multipliers whose combination is identically a negative
# constant, or zero with a strict part. That combination is the proof, replayed by a checker. No integer rounding is
# used, so every rule holds in every ORDERED FIELD, the real numbers included, though the variables are typed integer.
#
# Notation, as in metric: a distance d is carried by its cosh. For two sides of a triangle, p = cosh a and q = cosh b,
# with s = sinh a and t = sinh b given by their defining equations s^2 = p^2 - 1, t^2 = q^2 - 1 and signs s, t >= 0.
# Then cosh(a + b) = p q + s t and cosh(a - b) = p q - s t, and r = cosh c is the third side's.
#
#   ORDERED FIELD   the square-root step, the sign of a factor, products of signs;
#   ADDITION        (p q + s t, p t + q s) is again a (cosh, sinh) pair, so p q + s t IS cosh(a + b), and it is >= 1;
#   TRIANGLE        cosh(a - b) <= cosh c <= cosh(a + b), from the Gram determinant, and the upper bound is attained
#                   only when the Gram determinant is 0, which metric shows is det(x, y, z)^2: the points are collinear;
#   PASCH (II.4)    with u, v, w the signs B(a, n), B(b, n), B(c, n) of a triangle's corners against a line n missing
#                   c: if the line crosses a b (u v < 0) it crosses exactly one of a c and b c;
#   CONTINUITY      Tarski's elementary (line-circle) continuity, in the Klein disk: a line through a point inside a
#                   circle meets it, because the meeting point's quadratic has a non-negative discriminant;
#
# What this file does not give: CONTINUITY in Dedekind's second-order form, and the ARCHIMEDEAN property. Both hold
# for the real numbers and fail in some ordered fields, so no ordered-field proof can reach them. They need the real
# numbers as a complete ordered field, which the kernel does not have yet (note/term/hyperbolic-geometry.md). The
# meeting point itself is the root (-B + sqrt(disc)) / 2A, which exists in any field with square roots of its
# non-negatives (a Euclidean field). That existence is the one field axiom the continuity rule leans on.

# ORDERED FIELD. A squared inequality between a quantity and a non-negative one gives the inequality itself: from
# y >= 0 and x^2 <= y^2, x <= y. (With x = -2, y = 1 the sign hypothesis is what fails: it cannot be dropped.)
rule square-root-step
  mark x, like integer
  mark y, like integer
  have y-is-non-negative
    call is-minimum
      read y
      code 0
  have squares
    call is-maximum
      call multiply
        read x
        read x
      call multiply
        read y
        read y
  show hold
    call is-maximum
      read x
      read y

# A positive product with one positive factor has a positive other factor.
rule sign-of-a-factor
  mark a, like integer
  mark b, like integer
  have product-is-positive
    call is-above
      call multiply
        read a
        read b
      code 0
  have a-is-positive
    call is-above
      read a
      code 0
  show hold
    call is-above
      read b
      code 0

# Opposite signs multiply to a negative.
rule opposite-signs-give-a-negative-product
  mark a, like integer
  mark b, like integer
  have a-is-positive
    call is-above
      read a
      code 0
  have b-is-negative
    call is-below
      read b
      code 0
  show hold
    call is-below
      call multiply
        read a
        read b
      code 0

# ADDITION. (p, s) and (q, t) are points of the hyperbola X^2 - Y^2 = 1, and so is their product
# (p q + s t, p t + q s): the hyperbolic addition law, so p q + s t is the cosh of a distance, the sum of the two.
rule the-sum-of-two-distances-is-on-the-hyperbola
  mark p, like integer
  mark q, like integer
  mark s, like integer
  mark t, like integer
  have first-pair
    call is-equal
      call subtract
        call multiply
          read p
          read p
        call multiply
          read s
          read s
      code 1
  have second-pair
    call is-equal
      call subtract
        call multiply
          read q
          read q
        call multiply
          read t
          read t
      code 1
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call subtract
        call multiply
          call add
            call multiply
              read p
              read q
            call multiply
              read s
              read t
          call add
            call multiply
              read p
              read q
            call multiply
              read s
              read t
        call multiply
          call add
            call multiply
              read p
              read t
            call multiply
              read q
              read s
          call add
            call multiply
              read p
              read t
            call multiply
              read q
              read s
      code 1

# And it is at least 1, as a cosh must be: from p, q >= 1 (a cosh) and s, t >= 0.
rule the-cosh-of-a-sum-is-at-least-one
  mark p, like integer
  mark q, like integer
  mark s, like integer
  mark t, like integer
  have p-is-a-cosh
    call is-minimum
      read p
      code 1
  have q-is-a-cosh
    call is-minimum
      read q
      code 1
  have s-is-a-sinh
    call is-minimum
      read s
      code 0
  have t-is-a-sinh
    call is-minimum
      read t
      code 0
  show hold
    call is-minimum
      call add
        call multiply
          read p
          read q
        call multiply
          read s
          read t
      code 1

# THE TRIANGLE INEQUALITY, d(x, z) <= d(x, y) + d(y, z): cosh c <= cosh(a + b) = p q + s t. Its hypothesis is the
# Gram determinant's sign, (p^2 - 1)(q^2 - 1) - (r - p q)^2 >= 0, which metric proves for any three points (it is
# det(x, y, z)^2). Since cosh is increasing on the distances, this is the inequality of the distances.
rule the-triangle-inequality
  mark p, like integer
  mark q, like integer
  mark r, like integer
  mark s, like integer
  mark t, like integer
  have s-is-a-sinh
    call is-minimum
      read s
      code 0
  have t-is-a-sinh
    call is-minimum
      read t
      code 0
  have s-squared
    call is-equal
      call multiply
        read s
        read s
      call subtract
        call multiply
          read p
          read p
        code 1
  have t-squared
    call is-equal
      call multiply
        read t
        read t
      call subtract
        call multiply
          read q
          read q
        code 1
  have gram-is-a-square
    call is-minimum
      call subtract
        call multiply
          call subtract
            call multiply
              read p
              read p
            code 1
          call subtract
            call multiply
              read q
              read q
            code 1
        call multiply
          call subtract
            read r
            call multiply
              read p
              read q
          call subtract
            read r
            call multiply
              read p
              read q
      code 0
  show hold
    call is-maximum
      read r
      call add
        call multiply
          read p
          read q
        call multiply
          read s
          read t

# And the other side, cosh(a - b) = p q - s t <= cosh c: a side is at least the difference of the other two.
rule the-reverse-triangle-inequality
  mark p, like integer
  mark q, like integer
  mark r, like integer
  mark s, like integer
  mark t, like integer
  have s-is-a-sinh
    call is-minimum
      read s
      code 0
  have t-is-a-sinh
    call is-minimum
      read t
      code 0
  have s-squared
    call is-equal
      call multiply
        read s
        read s
      call subtract
        call multiply
          read p
          read p
        code 1
  have t-squared
    call is-equal
      call multiply
        read t
        read t
      call subtract
        call multiply
          read q
          read q
        code 1
  have gram-is-a-square
    call is-minimum
      call subtract
        call multiply
          call subtract
            call multiply
              read p
              read p
            code 1
          call subtract
            call multiply
              read q
              read q
            code 1
        call multiply
          call subtract
            read r
            call multiply
              read p
              read q
          call subtract
            read r
            call multiply
              read p
              read q
      code 0
  show hold
    call is-minimum
      read r
      call subtract
        call multiply
          read p
          read q
        call multiply
          read s
          read t

# EQUALITY: when cosh c = cosh(a + b) the Gram determinant is 0, which metric shows is det(x, y, z)^2, so the three
# points are collinear and y lies between: the triangle inequality is strict for every genuine triangle.
rule equality-in-the-triangle-inequality-is-degenerate
  mark p, like integer
  mark q, like integer
  mark r, like integer
  mark s, like integer
  mark t, like integer
  have s-squared
    call is-equal
      call multiply
        read s
        read s
      call subtract
        call multiply
          read p
          read p
        code 1
  have t-squared
    call is-equal
      call multiply
        read t
        read t
      call subtract
        call multiply
          read q
          read q
        code 1
  have equality
    call is-equal
      read r
      call add
        call multiply
          read p
          read q
        call multiply
          read s
          read t
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call subtract
        call multiply
          call subtract
            call multiply
              read p
              read p
            code 1
          call subtract
            call multiply
              read q
              read q
            code 1
        call multiply
          call subtract
            read r
            call multiply
              read p
              read q
          call subtract
            read r
            call multiply
              read p
              read q
      code 0

# PASCH'S AXIOM (II.4), the sign step. A line n crosses the side a b when B(a, n) and B(b, n) have opposite signs
# (u v < 0, and metric shows B is linear along the segment, so the crossing point is on it). With c off the line
# (w^2 > 0) it cannot cross both a c and b c: that would make u w < 0 and v w < 0, hence u v w^2 > 0, so u v > 0.
rule pasch-the-line-does-not-cross-both-other-sides
  mark u, like integer
  mark v, like integer
  mark w, like integer
  have crosses-a-c
    call is-below
      call multiply
        read u
        read w
      code 0
  have crosses-b-c
    call is-below
      call multiply
        read v
        read w
      code 0
  have c-is-off-the-line
    call is-above
      call multiply
        read w
        read w
      code 0
  show hold
    call is-above
      call multiply
        read u
        read v
      code 0

# And it crosses at least one: if it crosses a b and c is off the line, it crosses a c or b c.
rule pasch-the-line-crosses-one-other-side
  mark u, like integer
  mark v, like integer
  mark w, like integer
  have crosses-a-b
    call is-below
      call multiply
        read u
        read v
      code 0
  have c-is-off-the-line
    call is-above
      call multiply
        read w
        read w
      code 0
  show hold
    meet or
      call is-below
        call multiply
          read u
          read w
        code 0
      call is-below
        call multiply
          read v
          read w
        code 0

# CONTINUITY, line and circle (Tarski's elementary continuity). By homogeneity (hyperboloid) the circle's center is
# the origin of the Klein disk, where a circle of radius r is the Euclidean circle |k|^2 = m with m = tanh^2 r. The
# line through a = (a1, a2) in direction v = (v1, v2) meets it where |a + l v|^2 = m, a quadratic in l whose
# quarter discriminant is m |v|^2 - (a1 v2 - a2 v1)^2. When a is inside (|a|^2 <= m) it is >= 0, so the root exists.
# The proof is Lagrange's identity |a|^2 |v|^2 = d^2 + (a1 v2 - a2 v1)^2 with d = a . v, then |a|^2 <= m.
rule line-circle-continuity
  mark a1, like integer
  mark a2, like integer
  mark v1, like integer
  mark v2, like integer
  mark m, like integer
  mark d, like integer
  have a-is-inside
    call is-maximum
      call add
        call multiply
          read a1
          read a1
        call multiply
          read a2
          read a2
      read m
  have d-is-the-dot-product
    call is-equal
      read d
      call add
        call multiply
          read a1
          read v1
        call multiply
          read a2
          read v2
  show hold
    call is-minimum
      call subtract
        call multiply
          read m
          call add
            call multiply
              read v1
              read v1
            call multiply
              read v2
              read v2
        call multiply
          call subtract
            call multiply
              read a1
              read v2
            call multiply
              read a2
              read v1
          call subtract
            call multiply
              read a1
              read v2
            call multiply
              read a2
              read v1
      code 0
